% Canes and Crutches: LCD L33733 and its Policy Article A52459 (DME MACs). A
% policy program of the DMEPOS model: it includes dmepos.le (orders,
% delivery, benefit category, continued need, refills) and states when a
% claim for a cane (E0100, E0105) or a crutch (E0110, E0111, E0112, E0113,
% E0114, E0116) is REASONABLE AND NECESSARY: the criteria 1–3 the LCD states
% for all of them. The underarm, articulating, spring assisted crutch
% (E0117) is never covered ("the medical necessity ... has not been
% established"): no rule concludes it. The crutch substitute (E0118) the
% Policy Article describes ("either a device strapped to the lower leg with a
% platform or a device with wheels and a platform the beneficiary propels
% with their sound limb") has no criterion in the LCD, whose coverage
% statement names the canes and crutches by code without it: it is in scope
% and no rule concludes it either.
%
% How the criteria are read. The LCD's criteria are clinical findings about
% the beneficiary — a significant mobility limitation, that the beneficiary
% can safely use the cane or crutch, that a cane or crutch will sufficiently
% resolve the deficit — made by the treating practitioner and accepted or
% rejected on review: each is the judged template of dmepos.le, "the finding
% F about B is established" (or unsupported), with who found it and why. The
% findings this policy reads, and the criterion each stands for (the first
% is the finding pmd.le and walkers.le read for the same criterion; the
% others are named after pmd.le's "walking aids", its word for canes and
% walkers):
%   significant mobility limitation (1: a mobility limitation that
%   significantly impairs the ability to participate in one or more MRADLs
%   in the home — prevents the MRADL, or a heightened risk of morbidity or
%   mortality in attempting it, or prevents completing it within a
%   reasonable time frame); walking aid can be safely used (2: the
%   beneficiary is able to safely use the cane or crutch); walking aid
%   sufficiently resolves mobility deficit (3: the functional mobility
%   deficit can be sufficiently resolved by use of a cane or crutch).
%
% Not modelled: the white cane for a blind person, noncovered "since it is
% more of an identifying and self-help device", billed as A9270 — the
% generic noncovered code, claimed by seat_lifts.le; the coding of canes and
% crutches with springs or special weight capacity under the same codes
% (coding). The Policy Article adds no documentation requirement of its own.

the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
    % derived
    *an item* is a cane or crutch.
    *a beneficiary* meets the criteria for a cane or crutch.

the knowledge base canes_crutches includes these resources:
    dmepos.

the knowledge base canes_crutches includes:

LCD L33733 is published at "https://www.cms.gov/medicare-coverage-database/view/lcd.aspx?lcdid=33733".
the text of LCD L33733 is at "sources/lcd/L33733.txt".
Policy Article A52459 is published at "https://www.cms.gov/medicare-coverage-database/view/article.aspx?articleid=52459".
the text of Policy Article A52459 is at "sources/article/A52459.txt".

% What a cane or crutch with coverage criteria is: the codes the LCD's
% coverage statement names.
rule canes_crutches_item with provenance LCD L33733,
        confer "Canes (E0100, E0105) and crutches (E0110, E0111, E0112, E0113, E0114, E0116) are covered if all of the following criteria (1-3) are met":
an item is a cane or crutch
    if the HCPCS code of the item is a code
    and the code is in ["E0100", "E0105", "E0110", "E0111", "E0112", "E0113", "E0114", "E0116"].

% The codes this policy decides — outside the section checklist: a claim
% of another policy fails here without failing its own applicability.
% The codes this policy decides: the canes and crutches with criteria, the
% articulating crutch and the crutch substitute; their benefit, durable
% medical equipment.
rule canes_crutches_codes with provenance Policy Article A52459,
        confer "Canes and Crutches are covered under the Durable Medical Equipment benefit":
the policy for code a code is canes_crutches
    if the code is in ["E0100", "E0105", "E0110", "E0111", "E0112", "E0113", "E0114", "E0116", "E0117", "E0118"].

section applicability is:

canes_crutches has no specific documentation requirements.

rule canes_crutches_benefit with provenance Policy Article A52459,
        confer "Canes and Crutches are covered under the Durable Medical Equipment benefit":
the benefit category of code a code is durable medical equipment
    if the policy for code the code is canes_crutches.

section question is:

% A cane or crutch: criteria 1–3, all of them.
rule canes_crutches_covered with provenance LCD L33733,
        confer "Canes (E0100, E0105) and crutches (E0110, E0111, E0112, E0113, E0114, E0116) are covered if all of the following criteria (1-3) are met":
a claim is reasonable and necessary
    if the claim is for an item
    and the item is a cane or crutch
    and the item is furnished to a beneficiary
    and the beneficiary meets the criteria for a cane or crutch.

% Criteria 1–3: the findings of the treating practitioner.
rule canes_crutches_criteria_1_to_3 with provenance LCD L33733,
        confer "The beneficiary has a mobility limitation that significantly impairs his/her ability to participate in one or more mobility-related activities of daily living (MRADL) in the home":
a beneficiary meets the criteria for a cane or crutch
    if the finding significant mobility limitation about the beneficiary is established
    and the finding walking aid can be safely used about the beneficiary is established
    and the finding walking aid sufficiently resolves mobility deficit about the beneficiary is established.

% The articulating crutch (E0117) has no rule: "The medical necessity for an
% underarm, articulating, spring assisted crutch (E0117) has not been
% established; therefore, if an E0117 is ordered, it will be denied as not
% reasonable and necessary." Nor has the crutch substitute (E0118), for
% which the LCD states no criterion.
